{
  "abstract": "One round of GRPO on self-selected training questions lifts held-out sampled accuracy of a supplied Qwen3-0.6B-Base RLVR checkpoint by a few points. A path toward a substantially stronger model needs those rounds to compound. We test this prospectively. Round 2 reselects 1,024 questions that the round-1 policy solves 1-6 of 8 times among 4,000 fresh ones (32,000 rollouts) and repeats round 1's recipe from its endpoint. The gate was frozen and posted for critique before training, with two logged amendments. Held-out mean@8 on 1,000 never-monitored questions is 48.51 vs 49.20 for round 1 (-0.69 pp [-1.84, +0.44]): STALLS. Greedy on the same questions ties, 498 vs 498. Round 2 fits its own questions as round 1 did: training reward .43 to .57, greedy on the 1,024 trained questions +20.7 pp [17.0, 24.4]. Round 2 still moves held-out per-item success by about 80% as much as round 1 beyond a calibrated re-draw null, but without a sign: 205 items up, 210 down, against 313 up and 164 down for round 1. Round 1's signed gain is mostly what colder decoding gives the untrained start. T 0.4 adds +2.3 to +4.1 pp to the start and at most +1.2 after any training. Against the start at T 0.4, round 1 keeps +1.44 [0.05, 2.83] and round 2 +1.15 [-0.01, 2.34]. Round 1 also shrinks the mixed band that the next round selects from (.384 to .303). On a smaller monitored panel, round-2 greedy rises +7.7 items [-4.3, +20.0]. MATH-500 greedy, read once after the gate, is 252 vs 247 (+5 items [-13, +23]; start 227). Three more self-generated tail probes leave the never-sampled questions at their re-draw rate: grader myopia (0/234), the policy's own method plans (coverage -5 [-18, +8]), and an outlying expert-iteration seed. One start, one round-2 seed, 80-update rounds.",
